## Static Analysis

After decompiling the binary with ghidra we get the pseudo code below: 

```
void p(void)

{
  uint unaff_retaddr;
  char local_50 [76];
  
  fflush(stdout);
  gets(local_50);
  if ((unaff_retaddr & 0xb0000000) == 0xb0000000) {
    printf("(%p)\n",unaff_retaddr);
                    // WARNING: Subroutine does not return
    _exit(1);
  }
  puts(local_50);
  strdup(local_50);
  return;
}

void main(void)

{
  p();
  return;
}
```

We have two functions. </br>
The main function simply calls the `p` function. </br>
The `p` function stores `user input` in a buffer and checks whether the `return address` of the function is a `stack address` (i.e., within the range `0xb0000000 - 0xbfffffff`). </br>
If it is, the program triggers an exit condition. Otherwise, the buffer is duplicated and stored in the heap using strdup. </br>

## Vulnerability found 

The `gets` function writes user input from stdin into a buffer. However, gets does not check the length of the input (buffer overrun), which makes it a vulnerable function. <br/>
This allows us to write more bytes into memory than the buffer can hold, potentially leading to memory corruption.

## Dynamic Analysis

